.TH KSYMOFF 1 "2026-07-25" "kasld 0.3.1\-dev" "KASLD Manual"
.SH NAME
ksymoff \- apply a KASLR slide to kernel symbols and translate addresses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ksymoff
.RB [ \-b
.IR BASE " | " \-\-from
.IR NAME = ADDR ]
.BI \-s " SOURCE"
.RB [ \-\-slide ]
.RI [ SYMBOL " ...]"
.br
.B ksymoff
.BI \-\-page\-offset " BASE"
.RB { \-\-phys2virt
.IR ADDR " | " \-\-virt2phys
.IR ADDR }
.br
.B ksymoff
.BI \-\-vmemmap " BASE"
.RB [ \-\-page\-size
.IR N "] [" \-\-page\-shift
.IR N ]
.BI \-\-phys2page " ADDR"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ksymoff
applies a KASLR slide to kernel symbols, derives the slide and text base from a
known runtime symbol address, or translates physical and virtual addresses
through the direct map.
It works against
.IR System.map ,
.I /proc/kallsyms
snapshots, and a
.I vmlinux
ELF built with debug symbols.
.SS Modes
.TP
.B Forward
Given the runtime text base, print runtime addresses for one or more symbols,
or for every symbol in the source when none are listed.
.TP
.B Inverse
Given a known runtime symbol address
.RB ( \-\-from
.IR NAME = ADDR ),
derive the runtime text base. Combine it with positional symbol names to chain
into a forward lookup \(em one leaked address yields many.
.TP
.B Slide
With
.BR \-\-slide ,
print only the KASLR slide (signed hex). Combines with either mode above.
.TP
.B Physical
Translate physical and virtual addresses through the direct map, for data-only
pivots that operate on physical addresses. These need a base rather than a
symbol source.
.PP
The runtime text base is supplied in one of three ways, in priority order:
.B \-\-from
.IR NAME = ADDR ;
.B \-b
.IR BASE ;
or a
.B "kasld \-1"
line piped on standard input (the
.I text=
field is read).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " "\-\-base " \fIBASE\fR
Runtime text base (hex, with or without a
.I 0x
prefix).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " "\-\-symbols " \fIFILE\fR
Symbol source:
.IR System.map ,
a kallsyms dump, or a
.I vmlinux
ELF. Required for the symbol modes.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " NAME=ADDR"
Known runtime symbol address; derives the base. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-b .
.TP
.B \-\-slide
Print only the slide.
.TP
.BI \-\-page\-offset " BASE"
Direct-map base
.RI ( page_offset_base )
for
.B \-\-phys2virt
and
.BR \-\-virt2phys ,
or piped from
.B "kasld \-1"
(the
.I dmap=
field).
.TP
.BI \-\-phys2virt " ADDR"
Print the direct-map virtual address of a physical address.
.TP
.BI \-\-virt2phys " ADDR"
Print the physical address of a direct-map virtual address.
.TP
.BI \-\-vmemmap " BASE"
The vmemmap base (the
.I struct page
array), for
.BR \-\-phys2page .
.TP
.BI \-\-page\-size " N"
.IR sizeof(struct\ page) " (default: 64)."
.TP
.BI \-\-page\-shift " N"
Page shift,
.IR log2 (page\ size)
(default: 12, i.e. 4 KiB).
.TP
.BI \-\-phys2page " ADDR"
Print the
.I struct page *
for a physical address.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the full help and exit.
.SH REORDERED-TEXT KERNELS
.B ksymoff
assumes a single KASLR slide \(em a canonical, source/link-order function
layout, where every symbol sits at a fixed offset from the text base.
This is false on reordered-text kernels: under function-granular KASLR each
function is moved independently per boot (no single slide, so one leak pins
only that one symbol), and under LTO, AutoFDO, or Propeller the offsets come
from that exact build only (a generic version-level
.I System.map
mis-resolves).
Check the layout first with
.B "kasld \-H"
(the function-layout line) or the
.I text_order
field of
.BR "kasld \-H \-j" :
proceed only for a
.I canonical
layout; for a static reorder use the build_id-matched map; for a per-boot
reorder do not use
.B ksymoff
for chaining.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Forward \(em look up specific symbols:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ksymoff \-b 0xffffffff82200000 \-s System.map commit_creds
kasld \-1 | ksymoff \-s System.map commit_creds
.EE
.RE
.PP
Inverse \(em derive the text base from one known symbol, then chain:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ksymoff \-\-from commit_creds=0xffffffff82345678 \-s vmlinux \e
        prepare_kernel_cred init_cred modprobe_path
.EE
.RE
.PP
Translate physical to direct-map virtual, and a physical address to its
.IR "struct page" :
.PP
.RS
.EX
ksymoff \-\-page\-offset 0xffff9e8000000000 \-\-phys2virt 0x34600000
ksymoff \-\-vmemmap 0xffffea0000000000 \-\-phys2page 0x34600000
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The symbol source must correspond to the running kernel; a mismatch produces
wrong addresses without error. The
.B kernel_notes_buildid
component prints the GNU build_id from
.IR /sys/kernel/notes ;
.B readelf \-n
prints the build_id embedded in a
.IR vmlinux .
Equal values mean the
.I vmlinux
matches the running kernel.
.PP
.B ksymoff
requires
.BR bash (1)
for 64-bit hex arithmetic, and
.BR nm (1)
or
.BR readelf (1)
only when the symbol source is an ELF file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kasld (1),
.BR nm (1),
.BR readelf (1)
